"""Printed 289: source IVP and formation examples, with separate model clarification."""
from math12_reviewed_unit1 import heading,paragraph,page
from math12_reviewed_higher_derivatives import eq,note
FORMATION=[heading('10.2 Formation of Differential Equation'),paragraph('In most of the physical situations, we can observe the process but can not worked out directly to the differential equation. As a result, we have a general solution at our disposal before we know the equation of which it is the solution. Let’s begin with the step for forming with differential equation.'),paragraph('i. Discover the differential equation that describes a specified physical situation. ii. Find either exactly or approximately, the appropriate solution of that equation. iii. Interpret the solution that is found.'),paragraph('Look at the following examples for the concept of formation of differential equation.'),heading('Example 5'),paragraph('The rate at which the distance travels by Ali is 30 mph. Find the total distance travels by Ali at a time t hours.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('If S(t) is the unknown distance travel by you w.r.t. t number of hours, then, the rate at which the distance travels is the first derivative of S(t) with respect to t:'),eq(r'\frac{dS}{dt}=30,\quad\mathrm{the\ per\ hour\ speed}'),paragraph('Integrating with respect to t to obtain S(t):'),eq(r'\int\frac{dS}{dt}=\int30\,dt+c\quad\Rightarrow\quad S(t)=30t+c'),paragraph('S(t) is the distance travels by Ali with respect to t number of hours and the constant quantity c is the fixed distance in this situation.'),heading('Example 6'),paragraph('The rate at which the animal population is growing at a constant rate 4%. The habitat will support no more than 10,000 animals. There are 3000 animals present now. Find an equation that gives the animal population y w.r.t. x number of years.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('If P(x) is the unknown animal population w.r.t. x number of years, then, the rate at which the population grows is the first derivative of P(x) w.r.t. x:'),eq(r'\frac{dP}{dx}=k(N-P)=0.04(10,000-P)'),eq(r'\frac{dP}{10,000-P}=0.04\,dx'),paragraph('Here k=0.04 is the constant growth, N=10,000 is the total size of animal population in that habitat. Integrating with respect to x to obtain P(x):'),eq(r'\int\frac{dP}{10,000-P}=\int0.04\,dx+c'),paragraph('P(x) is the total population and c, the fixed population that depends on P=3000 when x=0. This problem is the IVP problem with the initial condition P(0)=3000.'),note('The printed equation models growth proportional to the unfilled capacity N−P, not a constant 4% of the current population. Following that equation gives P(x)=10000−7000e^(−0.04x), approaching capacity from below. Constant per-capita 4% growth would instead give P′=0.04P and would not respect the cap indefinitely; logistic growth is yet another model. The integration constant is fixed by the initial population rather than being the population itself. The source integral shorthand in Examples 4–5 means integrate both sides with respect to the independent variable.')]
IVP=[heading('Example 4'),paragraph('Determine a particular solution for the first order differential equation ds/dt=−32 ft/sec that satisfies the initial condition s=0, when t=0.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('This information develops the initial value problem ds/dt=−32, s(0)=0, whose unknown is the function s(t). Integrating directly with respect to t:'),eq(r'\frac{ds}{dt}=-32\quad\Rightarrow\quad\int\frac{ds}{dt}=\int-32\,dt+c\quad\Rightarrow\quad s(t)=-32t+c'),paragraph('The general solution s(t)=−32t+c at point P(0,0) gives c=0. Use this in the general solution to obtain the particular solution s(t)=−32t.'),note('Here s is signed displacement in feet and t is seconds; its derivative is −32 feet per second. This is constant velocity as printed. A displacement may decrease; a cumulative distance travelled cannot have this negative rate.')]
PAGES=[page('149_page_1.jpg',289,IVP+FORMATION)]
PAGES[0].update(title='DIFFERENTIAL EQUATIONS',visualStatus='Complete native IVP and formation models',alternateVerification={'pdf':'Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf','pdfPage':149,'side':'left','reason':'All rates, capacity, initial values and printed integral lines visually checked'})
EXAMPLES=[]
for n,q,steps,ans in [
('4','Solve ds/dt=−32 ft/sec with s(0)=0.',['Let s(t) denote signed displacement in feet and t time in seconds.','Integrate the constant derivative: s(t)=−32t+c.','Use the initial condition at t=0: 0=−32(0)+c.','Therefore c=0 and s(t)=−32t.','Differentiate: s′(t)=−32, satisfying the stated differential equation.','Also s(0)=0, satisfying the initial value. Negative velocity indicates decreasing signed displacement.'],'s(t)=−32t feet.'),
('5','Ali travels at 30 mph. Find distance S(t) after t hours.',['Take t in hours and S(t) in miles.','The constant speed gives dS/dt=30 miles per hour.','Integrate with respect to t: S(t)=30t+c.','The constant c is the initial distance reading S(0), which the question does not specify.','If S means distance travelled since starting at t=0, set S(0)=0, so c=0.','Verify S′(t)=30. Hence the general distance reading is 30t+c; newly travelled distance is 30t for t≥0.'],'S(t)=30t+c; with S(0)=0, S(t)=30t miles.'),
('6','Use the source animal-growth model with capacity 10000, rate 4%, and initial population 3000.',['Follow the printed equation P′=0.04(10000−P), with P(0)=3000.','For P≠10000, separate: dP/(10000−P)=0.04 dx. The equilibrium P=10000 is also a solution but fails this initial value.','Integrate: −ln|10000−P|=0.04x+C.','Since initially 10000−P=7000>0, the relevant branch gives 10000−P=Ae^(−0.04x).','Set x=0 and P=3000: A=7000. Hence P(x)=10000−7000e^(−0.04x).','Differentiate: P′=280e^(−0.04x)=0.04(10000−P); P(0)=3000.','For future x≥0, P increases from 3000 toward 10000. This is a capacity-gap model, whereas literal constant per-capita 4% growth has a different equation.'],'For the printed model: P(x)=10000−7000e^(−0.04x).')]:
 EXAMPLES.append({'num':n,'title':'Initial Values and Model Formation','question':q,'steps':steps,'answer':ans,'progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':289,'jpg':'149_page_1.jpg','verification':'All source rates and initial values visually checked; model distinction retained'}]})
